-- title:
--   The loop of the all-edges Exact Triangle host writes the marks of all pairs (aeLoop)
-- statement:
--   Correctness and running time of the main loop `aeLoop` of the all-edges host of Theorem 17.
--
--   Let a program $P_0\mathbin{+\!\!+}R$ contain a solver of Lop-AE-SparseTri (procedure $p_S$ of $P_0$ solves the detection task within $T_n$ steps under the need $\nu$) and the procedures `writeX`, `writeY`, `scanPairsAE` and `scan` at their numbers. Let $X$ be the data of a run of the host (sizes $n$, $D$, prime $p$, piece size, number $m$ of instances, the hashed weights, the pieces and the query pairs of every instance) and $A$ the addresses of its arrays, and assume that the data are consistent with the bound $U$, the memory $\mu$ holds the arrays of $X$ at $A$, the arrays are laid out without overlap, the limits allow all calls (including the need of the solver at every instance), the $n^2$ cells from $f_l$ that receive the marks lie apart from everything else, and they hold $0$ initially.
--
--   Then the body of `aeLoop`, run at depth $d$ on its arguments, ends within
--   $$t_{\mathrm{AELoop}}(T_n,X)=\sum_{t<m}\Big(t_{\mathrm{writes}}(n,D,\ell_t)+T_n[n,D,w_t]+t_{\mathrm{scanPairs}}(w_t,\ell_t,s_t)\Big)+14$$
--   steps (with $\ell_t$ the piece size, $w_t$ the number of query pairs and $s_t$ the number of scans of instance $t$), and the final memory agrees, below the address $A.x$, with $\mu$ in which the $n^2$ cells from $f_l$ hold the marks after all $m$ instances.
--
--   **Setting.** The all-edges host solves All-Edges Exact Triangle: given $n\times n$ integer weight matrices $w_{AB},w_{BC},w_{AC}$ with entries of absolute value at most $U$, it writes for every pair $(a,b)$ a flag that is $1$ exactly if some $c$ has $w_{AB}(a,b)+w_{BC}(b,c)+w_{AC}(a,c)=0$. As in the proof of Theorem 17 it chooses a prime $p\le\sqrt D$, hashes the weights, splits the third part into about $4ng$ pieces of at most $\lceil s/g\rceil$ vertices ($s=\lfloor\sqrt D\rfloor$), builds one Lop-AE-SparseTri instance per piece and residue class with at most $\lfloor n^2/\sqrt D\rfloor$ query pairs, calls a detection solver on each, and scans the candidate pairs it reports, skipping pairs that are already marked. The *marks* are the $n^2$ flags; after the last instance they are the answer.
--
--   The number of scans of an instance counts only pairs that are not yet marked, so over the whole loop the scans are at most the false positives plus $n^2$; this is what makes the all-edges variant cost no more than the decision version.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The statement is `Ends lim (P₀ ++ R) d (aeLoopBody pS pWriteX pWriteY pScanPairs) ⟨frame (aeLoopArgs X A fl), μ⟩ (tAELoop Tn X) (fun σ' => Kept (aeBase X fl μ X.m) σ'.mem A.x)`, under the hypotheses `AEHostCtx`, `HostOk X U`, `HostMem X A μ`, `HostLay X A`, `HostLim lim d X U A need`, `AEMarksLay X A fl` and the zero marks; the sum in the time is exactly `tAELoop`.
